From: theodotdot <59372255+theodotdot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:05:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] tools: define __s128 and __u128 in the tools' linux/types.h

The tools build puts tools/include on the include path, so <linux/types.h> resolves
to our copy instead of the system's. That copy has never carried the 128-bit
typedefs, and a host whose linux-api-headers are newer than the pinned kernel source
needs them: aarch64's <asm/sigcontext.h> describes SVE state with __u128.

  In file included from /usr/include/bits/sigcontext.h:30,
                   from /usr/include/signal.h:301,
                   from sigchain.c:2:
  /usr/include/asm/sigcontext.h:81:9: error: unknown type name '__u128'

Every host tool that includes <signal.h> then fails, which aborts `make kernel`
before anything is compiled. Seen building the pinned 7.2.10 on the Thor itself,
where linux-api-headers is 7.3; a cross build on x86 is unaffected because its
asm/sigcontext.h has no such type.

Copy the two typedefs the uapi header has, behind the same __SIZEOF_INT128__ guard.
---
--- a/tools/include/linux/types.h
+++ b/tools/include/linux/types.h
@@ -13,6 +13,11 @@
 #include <asm/types.h>
 #include <asm/posix_types.h>
 
+#ifdef __SIZEOF_INT128__
+typedef __signed__ __int128 __s128 __attribute__((aligned(16)));
+typedef unsigned __int128 __u128 __attribute__((aligned(16)));
+#endif
+
 struct page;
 struct kmem_cache;

